posted a new contract on whether Russia, Ukraine, and the European Union hold a trilateral diplomatic

00:09:22.513 --> 00:09:27.795
meeting by a date the contract names. A base rate is how often a class of

00:09:27.795 --> 00:09:32.455
similar cases already produced a given result, counted before the details of the

00:09:32.455 --> 00:09:36.595
case in front of you get a vote, and that counting habit is how it works as a

00:09:36.595 --> 00:09:41.915
forecast. Start with the frequency, then adjust, skip it, and you are doing what

00:09:41.915 --> 00:09:47.635
researchers call base rate neglect. An AAPG bulletin paper puts the miss on 25

00:09:47.635 --> 00:09:51.835
exploration wells, lunding petroleum drilled on the Norwegian Continental

00:09:51.835 --> 00:09:57.255
shelf, from 2011 through 2015. The class frequencies were the area's

00:09:57.255 --> 00:10:01.955
historical hit rate and the sizes of fields already found. Against those, the

00:10:01.955 --> 00:10:06.355
explorers consistently and significantly low-balled geological probability of

00:10:06.355 --> 00:10:11.635
success, and high-balled how big a hit would be. The fix the paper argues for is

00:10:11.635 --> 00:10:16.335
ordinary and easy to skip. Write the historical hit rate and the historical size

00:10:16.335 --> 00:10:21.535
range first. Put the prospect's own geology beside that record, not in place of it.

00:10:22.035 --> 00:10:27.095
A stack of careful case writeups will still miss the historical distribution if each one is allowed

00:10:27.095 --> 00:10:32.535
to forget the class. The author says explorers drop the frequencies because those frequencies do

00:10:32.535 --> 00:10:37.295
not feel like they are about this well, and that folding the old rates into the evaluation,

00:10:37.875 --> 00:10:43.755
rather than parking them in another file, is what shrinks the error. A 2024 paper on archive

00:10:43.755 --> 00:10:49.255
asks a harder question, what if the experts you are combining are neglecting the base rate themselves?

00:10:50.095 --> 00:10:55.115
Two people each hold a private signal and each state a probability for a yes or no world.

00:10:55.595 --> 00:11:01.855
A dial called Lambda runs from zero, no use of the base rate, to one, a perfect Bayesian update.

00:11:02.395 --> 00:11:07.735
The grade is worst case regret against an omniscient benchmark. The yardstick from Arieli,

00:11:07.735 --> 00:11:15.195
Babachenko and Smordinski in 2018. Regret traces of V. It does not simply fall as the experts get

00:11:15.195 --> 00:11:21.455
closer to perfect updating. A lambda short of 1 can beat fully Bayesian posterior on that worst

00:11:21.455 --> 00:11:26.815
case score, because the person combining the forecast does not know how the experts were informed.

00:11:27.375 --> 00:11:32.615
An extreme updated numbers can be the dangerous inputs in the worst case that combiner has to survive.

00:11:32.615 --> 00:11:38.635
The authors sketch a combiner built to stay low regret even when Lambda is unknown, and

00:11:38.635 --> 00:11:40.555
they say they tested the neglect model.

00:11:41.015 --> 00:11:44.895
A Journal of Cognitive Neuroscience Studies splits who catches the conflict.

00:11:45.435 --> 00:11:49.915
On the lawyer-engineer problem, the Cognitive Reflection Test tracked performance when

00:11:49.915 --> 00:11:52.615
the base rate and the stereotype pointed opposite ways.

00:11:53.315 --> 00:11:56.215
Short-term memory tracked the items where they did not conflict.

00:11:56.755 --> 00:12:01.935
In the scanner, conflict items lit the anterior single at more, even when the person still

00:12:01.935 --> 00:12:06.335
pick the stereotype. Higher reflection scores lined up with more posterior

00:12:06.335 --> 00:12:10.635
singular activity on those items, which the author's link to shifting attention

00:12:10.635 --> 00:12:15.715
from the outside queue to an internal check. That is one lab task, not a book of

00:12:15.715 --> 00:12:20.975
trades. Reference class forecasting is the same start in project work. A project

00:12:20.975 --> 00:12:24.795
management journal paper set forecast drawn from how similar projects

00:12:24.795 --> 00:12:28.835
actually finished against forecasts that tried to trace the path of the project

00:12:28.835 --> 00:12:33.275
in hand, including Monte Carlo simulation and earned value management.

00:12:33.895 --> 00:12:38.475
On real project data, the similar project's method won for both cost and time.

00:12:39.155 --> 00:12:42.475
The argument inside the stack is not that base rates are optional.

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:45.875
The Wells paper treats neglect as a fixable mistake.

00:12:46.475 --> 00:12:51.375
The aggregation paper says a perfect Bayesian number is not automatically the safest input

00:12:51.375 --> 00:12:54.675
when the combiner is blind to how the experts know what they know.

00:12:54.675 --> 00:12:59.335
A price on a contract is one case-level number from the people trading it.

00:12:59.755 --> 00:13:03.455
The base rate is the hit rate of the class that contract belongs to.

00:13:03.935 --> 00:13:09.175
If those two sit far apart, the useful question is which class the resolution rules actually

00:13:09.175 --> 00:13:13.815
put the contract in, and whether the traders started there or started from the story.

00:13:14.375 --> 00:13:17.835
That comparison is a reading habit, it is not a side to take.

